  "worked_solution": "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & \u27f9 & {u}^{\u2032}=1, \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t & \u27f9 & v=\\int {v}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{\u2032}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-\\pi ))+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\pi )+(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
